>Hmmm. Well, I think having an alignment is good because it forces you to play
>your character properly - without it we could just "do what we wanted to" which
>is not right when playing good characters at least.

There are two reasons why I don't like this alignment thing. Firstly,
it is quite arbitrary what is considered "lawful" and what is considered
"neutral". It's just some sort of ideology coming in here. Secondly, I
think it's unrealistic and rather silly to assume that some god is
watching your character's actions and gets pleased or pissed off by
them, and consequently decides whether to help that charecter etc. If
there should be consequences of certain actions, they should be of a
more real kind, such as Kops or watchmen attacking.
    If it is desired to take the consistency in the character's actions
into account, this could be done in the score. A record could be kept on
the character's good and evil actions, and the score could be modified
acoording to how consistent the character acted. A message regarding
this could be left on tombstones and in the highscore file.

>Having a AD&D system with nine alignments (Lawful Good, Lawful Neutral, Lawful
>Evil, Neutral Good etc) would be too complicated (and impossible to implement I
>think). Maybe the alternative could be 5 alignments:
> Lawful, Good, Neutral, Chaotic & Evil
>Although even this might be a bit difficult to work into the game properly.

I don't know AD&D, but this two-dimensional system is certainly much
more sensible than the one-dimensional we have in NetHack nowadays.

